# Function bodies

A function body is either one expression after `=`, or a **block** in
braces. Both forms declare the same kind of function, and either works for
any result type:

```hql
fn doubled_sum(xs: List<Int>) : Int = xs | map(x => x * 2) | sum

fn doubled_sum_block(xs: List<Int>) : Int {
    xs
    | map(x => x * 2)
    | sum
}

[doubled_sum([1, 2, 3]), doubled_sum_block([1, 2, 3])]
```

```text
[12, 12]
```

Use `=` for a body that fits on one line. Use a block when the body is
longer, or when naming intermediate values makes it clearer. Inside a block,
a pipeline can continue over several lines, each starting with `|`.

A block is one of two kinds, depending on whether it contains `yield`:

```d2
direction: down

block: "a block { ... }" {
  shape: diamond
}

value: "value block\nbindings, then one final expression\nthe result is that expression" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

iterating: "iterating block\nbindings, yield and for\nthe result is every value yielded" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

any: "any result type\nInt, String, List<T>, Seq<T>, ..." {shape: text}
seq: "Seq<T> or Stream<T>" {shape: text}

block -> value: "no yield"
block -> iterating: "contains yield"
value -> any
iterating -> seq
```

## The value block

A **value block** is a series of bindings, `name = expr`, one per line,
followed by one final expression. The value of the final expression is the
result of the function, and its type must be the declared result type:

```hql
fn label(pages: Int) : String {
    days = (pages + 39) / 40
    kind = "short" given pages < 200 otherwise "long"
    fmt("{} ({} days)", kind, days)
}

[145, 412] | map(label)
```

```text
["short (4 days)", "long (11 days)"]
```

Each binding names one step of the computation, and the final line combines
them. A value block can return a sequence too. Without `yield`, the last line
is the sequence:

```hql
fn even_squares(xs: List<Int>) : Seq<Int> {
    evens = xs | filter(x => x % 2 == 0)
    evens | map(x => x * x)
}

List(even_squares([1, 2, 3, 4]))
```

```text
[4, 16]
```

## Scope

A binding in a block can be used from the line after it to the end of the
block. A binding with the same name as one outside the block **shadows** it:
inside the block the name means the new value, and outside it keeps the old
one. A lambda on the last line can use the block's bindings:

```hql
k = 100

fn adder(n: Int) : Int -> Int {
    k = n * 10
    x => x + k
}

add = adder(2)
[add(1), add(5), k]
```

```text
[21, 25, 100]
```

The bindings of a block cannot be used outside the function:

```hql
fn area(w: Float, h: Float) : Float {
    a = w * h
    a
}
a
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `a` is not bound
 --> <expression>:5:1
  |
5 | a
  | ^
  = help: did you mean `at`?
```

## The iterating block

A block that contains `yield` is an **iterating block**. It can hold
bindings, `yield expr` and `for name in collection { … }`. Its result is the
sequence of values in the order the `yield`s produce them, so the declared
result type is `Seq<T>`, which ends, or `Stream<T>`, which need not:

```hql
fn countdown(from: Int) : Seq<Int> {
    for n in range(0, from) {
        yield from - n
    }
}

List(countdown(3))
```

```text
[3, 2, 1]
```

[[07-streams|Chapter 7]] covers iterating functions and the streams they
produce.

## Rules for blocks

A block ends either in a final expression or in `yield`s:

- A block of bindings alone has no value, and is rejected.
- A block that yields cannot also have an expression on a line of its own.
- `for` is allowed only in an iterating block.

The parser reports each of these:

```hql
fn one() : Int {
    x = 1
}

one()
```

```text
parse failure[unexpected-token]: a block ends in a value or yields; this one holds only bindings
 --> <expression>:1:16
  |
1 | fn one() : Int {
  |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

A block is written without `=`. After `=`, braces start a record or a set
literal, so `fn one() : Int = { x = 1 }` does not parse.

## Where functions are declared

A function has the same form wherever it is declared: at the top level of a
program, in a module ([[05-modules|chapter 5]]), as a receiver function that
takes `self` ([[receiver-modules|receiver modules]]), and in a trait or an
implementation ([[traits]]). A lambda's body is always one expression; a
function that needs a block is declared with `fn`.
